From 44b364bad521b30ee57dac795efe1186ec4c58df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iam <iam@iashur.internal>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 00:44:05 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] 360 viewer: fix loop-restart pacing bug (loopOffset
 overwritten, not accumulated)

Real bug, found by user testing stereo3d-pack conversions in the headset and
written up with real evidence in BUG_player_loop_speedup_2026-08-09.md
(stereo3d-pack repo): short clips would play fine through the first loop,
then blow up to a sustained ~3x speed right after the second loop-restart
and never recover.

Root cause: `loopOffset = lastPts + frameDuration` was a plain assignment,
not an accumulation. lastPts is always the file's own raw, 0-based last
timestamp - it naturally lands near the same value every loop, since the
file gets re-read from position 0 each time. The assignment silently threw
away every earlier loop's accumulated offset, so from the SECOND loop
restart onward every loop reused the exact same pts window the previous
loop used - a window that was already "in the past" relative to
playbackTime by the time that loop finished. The very next EOF/seek/flush
cycle then fired almost immediately (its own frames were stale the instant
they were queued), then the next one even faster, compounding into dozens
of loop-restarts per real second. Not slow motion, not a one-time jump: the
file being decoded and immediately discarded far faster than real time,
over and over, stuck in the same narrow pts window. Fixed by accumulating
(`loopOffset += ...`) instead of overwriting.

First hypothesis tried (this project's, before the real cause was found):
that AcquireCurrentSlot() not resetting clockStarted across the automatic
loop (unlike the manual-seek path, on purpose, to keep the wall clock
monotonic) was the whole story. Kept as a secondary fix alongside the real
one - it correctly discards any wall-clock drift accumulated during the
seek/flush itself, which the loopOffset fix doesn't address on its own -
but confirmed BY MEASUREMENT (live instrumented runs, see below) that it
alone was nowhere near sufficient: the bug still reproduced almost
identically with only that change in place.

Verified with live instrumented runs (temporary DEBUG logging, since
removed) against the exact repro from the bug report
(stereo3d-pack/out/media_sbs_lowres.mp4, 7.75s, 30fps, via play360.sh -p
flat -e sbs): confirmed loopOffset was pinned at one loop's width instead
of growing, confirmed queue.front().pts was already at the tail of the
SAME window playbackTime had just finished catching up to, and confirmed
loop-restart log lines going from ~7.5s apart to sub-second apart within a
couple of loops - directly matching the report's own measured 24fps ->
~3x transition. After the fix: 90s run (~12 loop crossings), 44 stat
samples, all 29.8-30.4 fps (file is 30.0), decode steady at 30.1 fps
throughout with 0 renderer starves. Not yet re-verified against the
report's SECOND symptom (a vertical "rolling" artifact on a different
clip) - unconfirmed whether that was a visible symptom of this same bug or
something separate; needs a look with the headset on.
---
 src/tests/hello_xr/video360.cpp | 32 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/tests/hello_xr/video360.cpp b/src/tests/hello_xr/video360.cpp
index 182ad70..08df22f 100644
--- a/src/tests/hello_xr/video360.cpp
+++ b/src/tests/hello_xr/video360.cpp
@@ -387,7 +387,37 @@ struct Video360::Impl {
                 DrainDecoder(frame, swFrame, loopOffset, lastPts);
                 if (!loop) break;
                 avcodec_flush_buffers(dec);
-                loopOffset = lastPts + frameDuration;
+                // BUG (found 2026-08-09, see BUG_player_loop_speedup_2026-08-09.md in the
+                // stereo3d-pack repo): this used to be a plain assignment, `loopOffset =
+                // lastPts + frameDuration`. lastPts is always the file's own RAW, 0-based
+                // last timestamp (it naturally restarts near 0 every loop, since the file is
+                // re-read from position 0 each time) - so the assignment silently threw away
+                // every earlier loop's accumulated offset and reused the SAME one-loop-width
+                // value forever after the second loop. From loop 3 onward every loop's frames
+                // were queued under the identical pts window loop 2 used, which was already
+                // "in the past" relative to playbackTime by the time loop 2 finished - so the
+                // very next EOF/seek/flush cycle would fire almost immediately (its own
+                // frames were stale the instant they were queued), then the next one even
+                // faster, compounding into dozens of loop-restarts per real second. That is
+                // the sustained ~3x speed-up: not slow motion, but the file being re-decoded
+                // and re-discarded far faster than real time, over and over, in the same
+                // narrow pts window. Confirmed live via temporary instrumentation logging
+                // loopOffset/queue state at every EOF - it stayed pinned at one loop's width
+                // instead of growing, and loop-restart log lines went from ~7.5s apart to
+                // sub-second apart within a couple of loops. Must accumulate, not overwrite:
+                loopOffset += lastPts + frameDuration;
+                {
+                    // Secondary, smaller effect, worth keeping regardless of the fix above:
+                    // re-anchor playbackTime to whatever's next in the queue instead of
+                    // trusting the wall-clock accumulator across the loop seam. Without this,
+                    // any real time spent in this seek/flush (however small) is wall-clock
+                    // time AcquireCurrentSlot() still counts as elapsed but that produced no
+                    // frames. Unlike the manual-seek path above, the queue is NOT cleared here
+                    // - whatever's still queued is the tail end of the loop that just
+                    // finished, still correctly paced and still due to be shown.
+                    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock2(mutex);
+                    clockStarted = false;
+                }
                 if (av_seek_frame(fmt, streamIndex, 0, AVSEEK_FLAG_BACKWARD) < 0) {
                     Log::Write(Log::Level::Warning, "video360: seek to start failed, stopping playback");
                     break;
-- 
